\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 \'93Damn! The ambulances are already there.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Apparently delta squad\'92s roadblocks failed.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander\'92s mask of passive agency finally cracked. He surged to the cockpit, hungry to take charge.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sir, safer in the cabin,\'94 warned the co-pilot.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m in charge here,\'94 Alexander replied, puffed up.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, you are,\'94 said Captain Junkers, the real boss, from the rear. \'93Right up until I give the orders.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander wilted. \'93I just want to see what\'92s going on.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Fine. Buckle up,\'94 said the pilot. \'93Last thing I want is your soft arse falling into my lap.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander took a jump seat and strained for a view.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A building facade was caving in, flames and smoke spewing from the ground floor. The blast radius was obvious, windows blown out for a hundred metres, street scorched, ground cars ablaze. Survivors were dragging themselves clear. More just lay still, clothes ragged, injuries cauterised or caked in ash.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Crowds of gawkers clustered just out of the burn zone.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Nowhere to land,\'94 the co-pilot said. \'93Unless you want me to take out a few ambos and patients.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Copy that,\'94 muttered Captain Junkers as he weighed up the pros and cons of landing on some soon-to-be corpses.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sir, I can cover it,\'94 said the sniper.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Constable Mark Sman came top of his academy class in almost everything. But in Demographically Sensitive Culling, he was on another level entirely. His scores were so sky-high that on graduation he was licensed to shoot almost anyone he pleased, his superiors trusting his professionalism implicitly to kill just the right mix of bystanders and keep the numbers clean. For instance, if Sman\'92s mission was to take out an able-bodied Caucasian male in his thirties, he could calculate to within a decimal point exactly how many others he\'92d need to kill across a statistically bulletproof proportion of gender, age, and ethnicity, so he and his masters could avoid accusations of prejudice of any kind. This, after all, was the sort of world in which, if you wanted to murder someone, you had to be able to prove you\'92d been perfectly fair about it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 For example, in the famous case of Purple Shirts versus the People of East Ulyssia, accusations that Sman was an anti-purple colourist were summarily tossed. His legal team simply submitted his cop-cam archives from the operation, which showed beyond doubt his killing spree wasn\'92t prejudiced against wearers of purple, or any other colour. Further, the footage confirmed Sman had not unduly targeted purple-wearing, tall, basketball-playing, balding women who hated mahjong or avoided afternoon tea.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>His skills became the stuff of legend, and police forces all over the world fought for his transfer. Sman, for his part, relished the demographic challenges in every region he worked. His demographically sensitive culling to cover the hit on the last train-spotting Inuit in Surrey\'97who also happened to like broken bicycles\'97was a career highlight. Still, after years of perfectly balanced spreadsheets, he grew homesick for East Ulyssia, occasionally confessing to close friends, \'93There are too many taxing Cloudborne where I come from.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He was nearly convicted once, when an audit showed he\'92d missed his quota in one key demographic. It was easily solved. Sman simply blew the head off the prosecutor, thereby balancing the books. Case dismissed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>So, when the cop with the sniper gun said, \'93Sir, I can cover it,\'94 you could trust he meant it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 The riot cops flung open the cabin doors as the pilot lowered the cruiser over the crush in the street. Pistols withdrew from holsters and aimed.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 \'93Only take out enough to set them moving,\'94 Constable Sman ordered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sman\'92s colleagues, who often accused him of pleasuring himself by creating pivot tables (they were clueless about their function), promptly emptied their magazines into the gawking throng.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sman swore. \'93Too many, you idiots!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The reaction was instant. Shrieks and howls pierced the air. The crowd undulated like snakes beneath a quilt, surging one way and then the other. Under their feet was a grisly crop circle of trampled bodies.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The pilot hovered while the riot cops jumped out, and corralled and arrested survivors. Sman sat in the doorway, legs swinging, rifle in hand, as the cruiser rotated for angles. As the riot cops on the ground did the heavy killing to balance the demographic numbers, Sman flicked up a panoramic hologram, faces and stats scrolling. He shot, got a green tick, shot again. Everything updating live.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93All targets acquired,\'94 called Junkers over the radio.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>But then something went wrong. Junkers radioed, \'93We had a Resist Arrest. Kramer shot a banker.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sman swore. \'93You\'92ve thrown the demo analysis. Starting again.\'94 He reset his charts, then beamed. \'93Ah! White hip hop artist.\'94 He pulled the trigger.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander frowned. \'93I don\'92t know stats, but wouldn\'92t killing from such a minority group require many more compensating kills?\'94 His small eyes narrowed, lips pursed, absently scratching behind one ear with soft, curved fingers.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Nah. Community service,\'94 Sman replied, ticking off face after face. \'93See?\'94 He paused and pointed to a floating graph. \'93Even the demos don\'92t care about white hip hop artists.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The pilot landed so the cops could load up their gagged and zip-tied \'93terrorists.\'94 The cruiser lifted off to give Sman more range. Sman kept shooting, checking his stats.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How much longer?\'94 Junkers asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Nearly right,\'94 Sman replied. \'93One more to be sure. Hover over the blast site.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander saw his moment. \'93Who\'92re you after?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Average Joe. White, thirties, blue collar. Labourer, gardener, cleaner\'85 you know.\'94 He scanned the digital casualty reports. The holo chimed: Chanta\'92s face.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander paled. Jesus! Chanta! He\'92d only rung him the night before for that suicide clean. Not a mate, exactly, but a drinking associate. Alexander scanned the blast zone.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Then Alexander saw Sarah collapsed, dust-caked, cut, unconscious or dead, but beautiful. His heart flipped. Choirs of angels sang to Alexander, and he fell in love.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta\'92s charred form lay beside her.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sman\'92s holoscreen chimed. He smiled. \'93There one is.\'94 Lined up for the kill on Chanta.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander kicked Sman\'92s rifle. The shot went wide, blowing an ambo tyre. Alexander did it again, knocking the rifle onto the skid below.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Junkers loomed over Alexander. \'93What in the Alfred Deakin was that?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sciatica. Old PR injury,\'94 Alexander muttered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Now angry, Junkers handed Sman his pistol. \'93Take the shot.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You can\'92t shoot him!\'94 Alexander said half-pleading, half-shouting.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Why the Edmund Barton not?\'94 Junkers pressed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander couldn\'92t out himself as Chanta\'92s mate. \'93They\'92re witnesses\'97I need them talking, not dead,\'94 he managed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Junkers\'92 eyes bulged. \'93I knew bringing you on this mission was a bad idea. Constable, take the shot.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sman took aim. Alexander shoved Junkers sprawling, then grabbed Sman by the shoulder and twisted him back inside the cruiser. Sman\'92s elbow struck the floor, and the pistol barked. The bullet, a grim little overachiever if ever there was one, entered Junkers at an orifice never before plugged with such ballistic force. Having taken the scenic route through the bowel, and shattering up his spinal column, the round erupted from the dome of his skull amid a fanfare of blood, bone, and what could only be described, once it had decorated the troop-cabin, as an unholy slushie of semi-digested egg salad. Even in the most sanctified halls of national defence, many would agree: a lifetime devoted to professionally covering one\'92s arse had ended so ironically. Junkers earned a cruel epitaph, if only for furthering the field of forensic investigation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander gasped and gagged, while Sman calmly pulled up his statical hologram and swiped tables, swirled pie-charts, dragged columns off-screen and stabbed at cells. After what seemed many drawn-out minutes of him conducting his symphony of numbers, Sman was rewarded with a green tick.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Roll the captain out of the cabin, lieutenant,\'94 Sman told the mortified Alexander. \'93The demographics never lie. Captain Average Joe Junkers was white, thirties\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He looked more like he was sixty,\'94 Alexander said, wiping the corner of his mouth.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sman shrugged. \'93In his thirties twice over, then. Whatever. He died labouring to clean this street up, which means I can juggle another dimension into the algorithm. I call that \'91Mission Accomplished\'92, lieutenant.\'94
